    Keywords: target-based drug discovery, phenotype drug discovery, precision medicine, generative AI, deep learning, machine learning, foundation model, single-cell RNA-seq
  </text>
  <heading level="2">
    <location value="107"/>
    <location value="154"/>
    <location value="195"/>
    <location value="162"/>
    1 Introduction
  </heading>
  <text>
    <location value="107"/>
    <location value="169"/>
    <location value="426"/>
    <location value="320"/>
    Drug discovery and development are among the most resource-intensive and high-risk endeavors in biomedical science. A new drug requires on average 10-15 years and over $ 1-2 billion to progress from initial concept to regulatory approval [1]. Despite substantial investment in target identification, lead optimization, and preclinical validation, approximately 90% of drug candidates that enter clinical trials ultimately fail [1, 2]. A major contributor to this high failure rate is the continued reliance on the onedrug-one-target paradigm in modern drug discovery [3, 4]. Although this approach has yielded important breakthroughs, particularly in oncology where actionable mutations drive targeted therapies [5], it fundamentally oversimplifies the complexity of most human diseases [6]. Chronic and systemic conditions such as neurological disorders, autoimmune diseases, metabolic syndromes, and substance use disorders are inherently polygenic, multifactorial, and dependent on cellular and physiological context [3, 4]. In such diseases, modulation of a single molecular node rarely restores homeostasis across the broader biological network, which may contribute to the particularly high attrition rates observed in these therapeutic areas [7]. Systems pharmacology and network-based drug discovery have emerged as alternative frameworks that emphasize the coordinated modulation of disease-relevant biological networks rather than isolated molecular targets [3, 6]. Yet despite their conceptual and therapeutic potential, relatively few AI-driven computational tools have been developed to implement these principles systematically and at scale, particularly for the de novo design of novel therapeutic compounds.
  </text>
  <text>
    <thread thread_id="2"/>
    <location value="107"/>
    <location value="322"/>
    <location value="426"/>
    <location value="378"/>
<![CDATA[Acomplementary and increasingly important goal in drug discovery is the design of therapies tailored to the individual patient. Inter-individual variability in disease biology, which arises from genetic background, epigenetic state, environmental exposures, and tissue-specific gene expression, means that population-level measures of drug response may obscure substantial differences in treatment efficacy and safety [8, 9]. The past decade has witnessed the rise of precision medicine, wherein patient-specific molecular profiles guide therapeutic decision-making [9]. However, precision drug design, defined here as the de novo generation of molecules tailored to a patient's]]>  </text>
  <page_break/>
  <text>
    <thread thread_id="2"/>
    <location value="86"/>
    <location value="47"/>
    <location value="405"/>
    <location value="81"/>
    specific molecular disease state, remains an unmet challenge. This limitation arises partly because conventional target-based approaches generally do not incorporate the cellular and transcriptomic context that distinguishes individual patients, and partly because computational frameworks capable of integrating patient-specific omics data with generative molecular design in a scalable manner remain limited.
  </text>
  <page_footer>
    <layer value="furniture"/>
    <location value="264"/>
    <location value="393"/>
    <location value="269"/>
    <location value="398"/>
    2
  </page_footer>
  <page_header>
    <layer value="furniture"/>
    <location value="47"/>
    <location value="4"/>
    <location value="462"/>
    <location value="19"/>
    made available under a CC-BY-NC 4.0 International license. (which was not certified by peer review) is the author/funder, who has granted bioRxiv a license to display the preprint in perpetuity. It is bioRxiv preprint doi: https://doi.org/10.64898/2026.07.17.739269; this version posted July 22, 2026. The copyright holder for this preprint
  </page_header>
  <text>
    <location value="86"/>
    <location value="83"/>
    <location value="405"/>
    <location value="220"/>
    The advent of transcriptomics and, more recently, single-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq) has substantially expanded our ability to characterize disease processes at cellular resolution. By profiling the gene expression of thousands of individual cells simultaneously, scRNA-seq reveals cell type-specific transcriptional programs that are invisible to bulk sequencing approaches, enabling identification of novel disease-relevant cell subpopulations, drug targets, and biomarkers [10]. This cellular resolution is particularly critical for studying heterogeneous diseases, in which distinct cell populations may exhibit different pathological states and responses to therapeutic perturbations [10]. The use of scRNA-seq for personalized and cell type-specific drug discovery, in which therapeutic molecules are designed to shift disease-associated cellular states toward healthy states, provides an alternative to conventional target-centric drug discovery [11, 12]. Because this approach does not require prior identification of a specific molecular target, it is particularly applicable to diseases with complex or incompletely understood molecular mechanisms, including many chronic and systemic conditions [11, 12]. Nevertheless, compared with target-based drug discovery, relatively few computational methods have been developed for systems pharmacology approaches centered on the modulation of cellular states [13-16], and the integration of single-cell transcriptomics with de novo molecular generation remains a largely open problem [16].
  </text>
  <text>
    <thread thread_id="3"/>
    <location value="86"/>
    <location value="222"/>
    <location value="405"/>
    <location value="373"/>
<![CDATA[The emergence of foundation models pretrained on large-scale biological data has provided new computational tools for addressing this challenge. scRNA-seq foundation models [17, 18] have demonstrated the capacity to learn rich, generalizable representations of gene regulatory networks and cellular states through self-supervised learning, enabling strong transfer performance across a range of downstream biological tasks. In parallel, generative models for molecular design have advanced rapidly. Variational autoencoders [19], generative adversarial networks [20], transformer-based chemical language models [21, 22], and equivariant diffusion models [23] have each demonstrated the capacity to generate novel, drug-like molecules with desired physicochemical and biological properties, greatly accelerating the exploration of chemical space relative to traditional high-throughput screening [24]. Several recent works have begun to bridge transcriptomics and molecular generation. MolGAN [13] pioneered this direction by conditioning a generative adversarial network on bulk transcriptomic signatures to generate hit-like molecules with desired gene expression effects, demonstrating the feasibility of phenotype-guided molecular design. GxVAEs [14] extended this concept by employing two jointly trained variational autoencoders, one to extract latent features from bulk gene expression profiles and another to generate drug-like molecules conditioned on those features. GexMolGen [15] further advanced the field by adopting a cross-modal 'first-align-then-generate' strategy that leverages a scRNA-seq foundation model (specifically scGPT) to encode bulk gene expression signatures and align them with a scaffold-based molecular decoder, yielding hit-like molecules with]]>  </text>
  <page_break/>
  <text>
    <thread thread_id="3"/>
    <location value="107"/>
    <location value="47"/>
    <location value="426"/>
    <location value="81"/>
    improved structural similarity to known active compounds. Most recently, MolGeneE [16] made a critical step toward single-cell resolution by combining a cross-modal domain adaptation model to harmonize bulk and single-cell chemical transcriptomics data with a contrastive learning-based generative model, demonstrating that single-cell omics data can, in principle, guide molecular generation.
  </text>
  <page_footer>
    <layer value="furniture"/>
    <location value="243"/>
    <location value="393"/>
    <location value="248"/>
    <location value="398"/>
    3
  </page_footer>
  <page_header>
    <layer value="furniture"/>
    <location value="47"/>
    <location value="4"/>
    <location value="462"/>
    <location value="19"/>
    made available under a CC-BY-NC 4.0 International license. (which was not certified by peer review) is the author/funder, who has granted bioRxiv a license to display the preprint in perpetuity. It is bioRxiv preprint doi: https://doi.org/10.64898/2026.07.17.739269; this version posted July 22, 2026. The copyright holder for this preprint
  </page_header>
  <text>
    <location value="107"/>
    <location value="83"/>
    <location value="426"/>
    <location value="220"/>
    Despite these advances, existing transcriptomics-guided molecular generation methods retain several important limitations. First, MolGAN and GxVAEs rely exclusively on bulk transcriptomics data, primarily the L1000 database, which lacks the cellular resolution needed to distinguish disease-associated states across individual cell types and therefore limits the generation of molecules tailored to specific cell populations within a patient. Second, GexMolGen and MolGene-E employ two-stage frameworks in which transcriptomic and molecular representations are learned separately and subsequently aligned through contrastive learning. Because the integration of the two modalities is primarily achieved through representation alignment rather than continuous interaction throughout molecular generation, these approaches may be limited in their ability to capture cell-state-dependent chemical-gene relationships. Third, existing methods are not explicitly designed to preserve cell type-specific transcriptomic differences during molecular generation and have not systematically demonstrated that distinct cellular states lead to correspondingly distinct molecular outputs. Finally, none of these approaches has been evaluated for personalized drug discovery in patient-derived cohorts, nor have they demonstrated robust generalization to previously unseen cell types. These capabilities are important for extending transcriptomics-guided molecular generation to heterogeneous diseases and clinically relevant settings.
  </text>
  <text>
    <location value="107"/>
    <location value="222"/>
    <location value="426"/>
    <location value="322"/>
    Given these limitations, none of these methods have been applied to single cellbased drug discovery. Opioid use disorder (OUD) provides a clinically important example of the need for personalized and cell type-specific therapeutic design. The ongoing opioid epidemic remains a major public health crisis, yet pharmacological treatment options for OUD remain limited, and relapse and inadequate treatment response are common [25]. Emerging single-cell transcriptomic studies have revealed that opioid exposure induces heterogeneous transcriptional changes across distinct neuronal and glial cell populations within brain reward circuits, and that these responses vary substantially across patients [26, 27]. This heterogeneity at both the patient and cell type levels suggests that uniform pharmacological strategies may not adequately address the diverse molecular states associated with OUD. Personalized therapeutic design based on individual, cell type-specific transcriptomic profiles may therefore provide a more direct approach for identifying compounds capable of modulating disease-associated cellular states.
  </text>
  <text>
    <thread thread_id="4"/>
    <location value="107"/>
    <location value="324"/>
    <location value="426"/>
    <location value="373"/>
    Here we introduce GEM-GPT ( G ene E xpression-guided M olecular G enerative P re-trained T ransformer), a single cell transcriptomics-guided molecular generation framework designed to address these limitations through three main contributions. First, GEM-GPT employs a biology-inspired deep fusion strategy that integrates scRNA-seq foundation model representations with molecular token representations at each hidden layer of a unified generative architecture. This design enables continuous interaction between cellular-state and molecular representations during
  </text>
  <page_break/>
  <text>
    <thread thread_id="4"/>
    <location value="86"/>
    <location value="47"/>
    <location value="405"/>
    <location value="132"/>
    generation, supporting more detailed modeling of cell-state-dependent chemical-gene relationships than alignment-based approaches. Second, by conditioning molecular generation directly on cell type-aware disease-state representations derived from a pretrained single cell foundation model, GEM-GPT generates personalized and cell type-specific candidate molecules predicted to shift disease-associated transcriptional states toward healthy phenotypes. It produces distinct molecular outputs across cell types, generalizes to previously unseen cell types, and consistently outperforms existing state-of-the-art baselines across multiple evaluation settings. Third, through its application to OUD, GEM-GPT identifies diverse candidate molecules targeting cell type-specific disease states in individual patients, including FDA-approved drugs relevant to OUD, establishing a new paradigm for AI-driven, omics-based, personalized drug discovery.
  </text>
